def e(a,c,r,s):edit(m,a,c,r,s)
def f(i,k,c,r,s,o=None):qe(q,'io-models-q'+str(i),k,c,r,s,o)
e('## Two phases of every input operation','## A two-phase model of ordinary socket input','Zero-copy/direct/EOF paths differ.',rd)
e('The key insight is that a read has two distinct phases:','For an ordinary buffered socket read, use this simplified two-phase model:','Limit pedagogical model.',rd)
e('Phase 2 is a memory copy: microseconds, but not free.','Phase 2 involves copying with a cost dependent on size, memory state and implementation; it has no fixed microsecond duration.','Unmeasured timing omitted.',rd)
e('The default. `read()` puts the calling thread to sleep until data arrives *and* has been copied.','For a blocking stream socket, a positive-length `read()` can wait for data, EOF or an error. Available data may return immediately, possibly as a short read; copying is completed before the call returns.','Read not always sleep or wait fullrequest.',rd)
e('It works well up to a few thousand connections, then hurts:','Its costs depend on the workload and resource limits:','No universal capacity cutoff.',th)
e('8 MB of virtual address space by default on Linux, though only touched pages use RAM','size determined by thread attributes and runtime limits, not a universal Linux 8 MB default; reserved address space differs from resident memory','Stack limit variable.',th)
e('Thousands of threads mean lots of context switches and scheduler work.','Runnable threads and wakeups add scheduler work; idle sleeping threads mainly consume memory and bookkeeping resources.','Thread count alone not context-switch rate.',th)
e('If no data is ready, it fails immediately with `EAGAIN` (also spelled `EWOULDBLOCK`).','For supported socket operations with no available data, EOF or other result, it reports `EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK`. Portable code checks both; these constants need not be equal on every platform.','Portable EWOULDBLOCK distinction.',rd)
e('fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, fl | O_NONBLOCK);','if (fl == -1) { perror("F_GETFL"); exit(1); }\nif (fcntl(fd, F_SETFL,\n          fl | O_NONBLOCK) == -1) {\n    perror("F_SETFL"); exit(1);\n}','Check setup error rather than possibly block.',op)
e('if (n < 0 && errno == EAGAIN) {','if (n < 0 && (errno == EAGAIN ||\n              errno == EWOULDBLOCK)) {','Portable would-block check.',rd)
e('On its own, non-blocking I/O leads to **busy-polling**:','A naive use of non-blocking I/O can lead to **busy-polling**:','Not inevitable behavior.',rd)
e('so that a spurious wake-up or a read larger than what\'s available doesn\'t stall the whole loop.','because readiness can become stale or be spurious. An ordinary stream read need not wait to fill its buffer, but flags such as MSG_WAITALL and concurrent readers require care.','Large ordinary read alone not forced fullbuffer wait.',rd)
a=Path(m).read_text();st=a.index('It is rarely used for TCP.');ed=a.index('\n\n## 5.',st)
e(a[st:ed],'Standard SIGIO notifications can coalesce, so programs must re-check descriptor state rather than count signals as events. Signal handlers have async-signal-safety restrictions. Linux `F_SETSIG` with `SA_SIGINFO` can include descriptor/event information, and real-time signals can queue subject to resource limits; fallback to SIGIO is possible. Multiplexing often makes per-descriptor event handling easier.','Incorrect categorical lack of information/queueing and unsourced event/prevalence list.',sig)
e('Tell the kernel: "read into this buffer and tell me when it\'s **done**". The call returns immediately; the kernel does both phases; you are notified when the data is already in your buffer.','Request "read into this buffer and report when it is **done**". A completion interface separates submission from collecting the result. The operation may finish before submission returns or later, and the implementation may use kernel machinery or helper threads. Submission itself still has cost and possible errors.','API contract distinct from implementation/immediate return.',ai)
e('cb.aio_offset = 0;','cb.aio_offset = 0;\ncb.aio_sigevent.sigev_notify = SIGEV_NONE;','Explicit no-signal completion mode.',ai)
e('aio_read(&cb);         /* returns now */','if (aio_read(&cb) == -1) {\n    perror("aio_read"); exit(1);\n}','Check enqueue failure.',ai)
e('ssize_t n = aio_return(&cb);','int status = aio_error(&cb);\nssize_t n = aio_return(&cb);\n/* status: completion error, or 0. */','Collect operation error as well as byte result.',ai)
e("Linux's older native AIO (`io_submit`) is only reliably asynchronous for files opened with `O_DIRECT`, and can silently block otherwise. Real, general-purpose completion-based I/O on Linux arrived with **io_uring** in 2019 (lesson 4).","Linux native AIO (`io_submit`) has operation/filesystem limitations, and submission can block even with direct I/O in some paths. **io_uring**, introduced in Linux 5.1 in 2019, provides a broader completion interface; it can also use worker threads and has operation-specific support rules (lesson 4).",'O_DIRECT not sufficient no-block guarantee; avoid real-vs-fake async.',ai)
e('| Asynchronous | Doesn\'t block | Kernel does it |','| Asynchronous interface | Separate submission/completion | Implementation completes it |','Helper thread implementation allowed.',ai)
e('because the process blocks during the copy. Only model 5 is truly asynchronous.','because the calling thread cannot use the returned result until the read finishes. Here synchronous does not mean the scheduler necessarily puts that thread to sleep during copying. Model 5 instead separates submission and completion.','Synchronous versus scheduler-blocked terminology.',ai+rd)
e('| Works for files | No | Yes |','| Ordinary regular files on Linux | Readiness is not storage completion | Supported operations can report completion |','Kqueue/files and readiness distinction.',op)
e('| Syscalls per read | 2 (wait + read) | Can batch |','| Syscall costs | Waits can cover many reads | Submission/completion can batch |','Not fixed two syscalls per read.',rd)
e('**Asynchronous I/O in the POSIX sense**: the kernel completes the operation while you do something else (model 5).','**Asynchronous I/O interface**: completion is reported separately from submission; helper threads or kernel facilities may do the work (model 5).','No kernel-only requirement.',ai)
e("Python's `asyncio` and Node.js are built on readiness-based multiplexing (epoll, kqueue) underneath, plus thread pools for things like files and DNS.","On Unix, default selector-based asyncio and libuv commonly use readiness APIs for sockets, with worker mechanisms for some blocking work. Windows uses different backends: Python's default ProactorEventLoop and libuv use IOCP. Runtime version and event-loop choice matter.",'Platform-dependent backend.',py+uv)
e('So when someone says "Node.js does async I/O", they mean the third: your code doesn\'t block, because libuv multiplexes sockets with epoll and quietly runs file reads on worker threads. At the kernel level, the socket reads are synchronous, non-blocking calls.','For Node.js on a typical Unix libuv backend, asynchronous JavaScript APIs can sit above non-blocking socket readiness and worker-based file operations. That does not make every operation universally non-blocking or every platform use epoll; synchronous APIs and CPU-heavy callbacks can still block the event loop.','Not universal Node backend or no-block API.',uv)
e('- **High-throughput storage or very high packet rates on Linux**: completion-based I/O with io_uring.','- **Linux workloads needing completion interfaces or batching**: evaluate supported io_uring operations against a measured baseline.','No universal performance recommendation.',ur)
e('- **Avoid**: signal-driven I/O for TCP, and assuming POSIX AIO on Linux is "real" async.','- **Implementation matters**: glibc POSIX AIO has asynchronous completion semantics even though workers perform the I/O; signal-driven designs require careful state and signal handling.','Thread based still real asynchronous interface.',ai+sig)
e('- Every read has two phases: waiting for data to be ready, then copying it from kernel to user space.','- The two-phase socket-read model separates waiting for readiness from copying; it is a teaching model, not every possible I/O path.','Recap scope.',rd)
e('- Blocking, non-blocking, multiplexing and signal-driven I/O all block during the copy, so by POSIX\'s definition they are synchronous. Only asynchronous I/O completes both phases without the process.','- In the first four models, the caller performs a read and receives its result when that call finishes. Completion interfaces separate submission from result collection.','Avoid scheduler-blocked and no-process claim.',ai)
e('- "Async" in Node.js or `asyncio` usually means non-blocking sockets plus an epoll or kqueue event loop, not kernel asynchronous I/O.','- Language async/await does not specify the underlying I/O mechanism; runtime, platform and operation determine readiness, completion or worker-based behavior.','Platform recap.',uv+py)
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Deployment evidence gap\n> No benchmark establishes connection-count limits, universal stack residency, copy latency or a fastest I/O model here. Numeric scenarios are assumptions; exact costs and runtime backends must be checked for the deployed version. C examples are fragments requiring headers, initialized descriptors/buffers and lifecycle cleanup. Keep asynchronous buffers/control blocks valid until completion.\n\n## Further reading','Visible omission and code-fragment prerequisites.',ai+th)
f(1,'prompt','A positive-length blocking `read()` on an established TCP socket returns `0`, with no local receive-side shutdown. What does this indicate?','Zero-length read and local shutdown exceptions.',rd)
f(1,'explanation','Absent EOF or errors, a blocking positive-length read can wait for data. In non-blocking mode an empty open stream normally reports EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK.','Avoid neverreturns early absolute.',rd,0)
f(1,'workedExample','For the stated positive-length stream read, a positive return reports bytes, zero indicates receive EOF, and −1 reports an error. After buffered bytes are consumed, a peer FIN produces EOF; the peer may have half-closed only its sending direction. A zero-byte read request is a different case and need not mean EOF. Do not busy-retry EOF as though it were EAGAIN.','EOF scope and half-close.',rd)
f(2,'prompt','Consider this Python snippet after a successful connection. The server keeps its sending side open, sends no data, and no reset or other error occurs.\n\n```python\ns = socket.create_connection(\n    (host, port))\ns.setblocking(False)\ntry:\n    print(s.recv(100))\nexcept BlockingIOError:\n    print("later")\n```\n\nWhat does it print?','Explicit open-silent peer assumptions.',rd)
f(3,'explanation','Correct in the simple no-race model. The readiness wait covers many descriptors, followed by a separate read. Use non-blocking I/O because readiness can change before that read.','Readiness not guarantee under race.',rd,3)
f(4,'workedExample','1. epoll reports readiness; a later read performs the copy and returns the result to its caller.\n2. The thread can execute kernel copy instructions rather than being asleep in the scheduler; synchronous describes the operation contract.\n3. Completion interfaces separate submitting an operation from learning its result.\n4. They may use kernel execution or workers, and an operation can finish before submission returns.','Synchronous not same as sleeping/kernel-only completion.',ai)
f(5,'prompt','On Linux, a server sets O_NONBLOCK on an ordinary regular file, then uses read() in its event loop. Tracing shows some reads waiting for storage on page-cache misses. Why does O_NONBLOCK not prevent those waits?','Trace-supported cause and Linux scope.',op)
f(5,'explanation','O_NONBLOCK does not make ordinary regular-file reads return EAGAIN on page-cache misses. Other operations or filesystem situations may produce EAGAIN, so never is too broad.','No universal never EAGAIN.',op,1)
f(5,'explanation','O_DIRECT is not required to read a file and does not turn a synchronous read into an asynchronous operation. Direct-I/O support and behavior vary by filesystem and alignment.','Direct I/O not everyreadalwaysphysical/slower.',op,3)
f(5,'workedExample','1. Linux O_NONBLOCK does not provide asynchronous page-cache-miss handling for ordinary regular-file read().\n2. Readiness APIs are not storage completion notifications for those files.\n3. If the event-loop thread waits for storage, unrelated callbacks wait too.\n4. Move supported work to a worker pool or a suitable completion interface. Actual latency and backend support must be measured, not inferred from a generic SSD/disk range.','Remove unverified timing and dataalways exists claim.',op+uv)
f(6,'prompt','Which interfaces can report completion separately from submitting an I/O request, regardless of whether the implementation uses kernel work or helper threads? Select all that apply.','Question previously excluded its own glibc correct answer.',ai)
f(6,'explanation','Correct for submitted read/write operations. io_uring also has readiness-poll operations; a CQE must be interpreted according to its opcode and result.','CQE not always payload completed read.',ur,2)
f(7,'prompt','What complicates a conventional O_ASYNC + standard SIGIO design for a TCP server?','Avoid unsupported prevalence claim.',sig)
f(7,'text','Standard SIGIO notifications can coalesce, the application must re-check socket state, and signal handlers have safety restrictions','Avoid cannotidentifyanyevents absolute.',sig,3)
f(7,'explanation','Correct. Linux extensions can supply descriptor/event information and queue real-time signals, but queue limits and fallback to SIGIO must still be handled.','F_SETSIG information and queued realtime support.',sig,3)
f(7,'workedExample','1. Standard SIGIO signals can coalesce; one delivered signal is not one counted socket event.\n2. The application must inspect current descriptor state.\n3. Handler code must obey async-signal-safety rules.\n4. F_SETSIG plus SA_SIGINFO can provide descriptor/event details, and real-time signals can queue up to resource limits. Overflow can revert to SIGIO.\n5. Readiness multiplexing can simplify collecting per-descriptor events, though events still require correct state handling.','Correct current Linux signal capabilities.',sig)
f(8,'prompt','Assume a Linux thread-per-connection server has 20,000 threads and explicitly configures an **8 MiB** stack reservation per thread. Ignoring guard pages and other mappings, what virtual stack reservation results, and must it all be resident?','Not universal default; binary arithmetic.',th)
f(8,'text','156.25 GiB of virtual stack reservations; resident usage depends on touched/resident pages','Correct binary result.',th,1)
f(8,'explanation','Correct. 20,000 × 8 MiB / 1024 = 156.25 GiB. This excludes guards and other memory. Thread creation can still hit process/resource limits; address-space arithmetic does not guarantee successful creation.','Capacity not guaranteed all64bit.',th,1)
f(8,'text','156.25 GiB, necessarily all resident immediately','Align wrong option units.',th,2)
f(8,'workedExample','1. 20,000 × 8 MiB = 160,000 MiB = 156.25 GiB.\n2. Reserved virtual space and physical residency differ; neither this arithmetic nor the thread count determines actual resident memory.\n3. If one additionally assumes 32 KiB resident per stack, the model gives 625 MiB, but no measured stack profile is supplied.\n4. Thread limits, kernel metadata, guards and wakeup patterns add costs. Event loops reduce the need for a dedicated waiting thread per connection, not all scheduling/memory costs.','Fix mixed MB/GiB and unsupported typical residency.',th)
f(9,'prompt','With CPython\'s default selector-based asyncio loop on Linux, suppose a stream coroutine awaits data that is not yet buffered. Which description matches the readiness path?','Specify backend and absentbuffer; notevery await suspends.',py)
f(9,'explanation','Explicit blocking work can use an executor, and some built-in operations such as name resolution also use it. In this default selector socket-read path, receiving data occurs through readiness handling.','Executor not onlyexplicit calls.',py,1)
f(10,'text','A naive design that keeps one pending receive per idle connection must retain each supplied buffer until that operation finishes','Buffer lifetime not necessarily page pinning.',ur,0)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. If each of 100,000 requests retains an assumed 16 KiB buffer, their buffer capacity totals 1,562.5 MiB. This is not automatically all resident or pinned. Provided buffers can reduce dedicated per-connection retention, but a shared pool still consumes resources.','Correct units and pinning distinction.',ur,0)
f(10,'explanation','For ordinary Linux regular files, readiness does not tell when storage I/O will finish; select/poll commonly report ready and epoll rejects unsupported file types. This is not a universal claim that readiness APIs have no file-related functions.','Platform/file distinction.',op,3)
f(10,'workedExample','1. A pending receive using a supplied buffer requires that buffer to remain valid until completion.\n2. Assumed capacity: 100,000 × 16 KiB = 1,638,400,000 bytes = 1,562.5 MiB. Residency and pinning depend on API/setup.\n3. A readiness design can obtain a buffer when attempting the read.\n4. io_uring provided-buffer groups share a pool; availability, ownership and replenishment must be handled. Pool memory is still allocated.\n5. Ordinary regular-file readiness on Linux is not storage completion; worker or completion mechanisms can avoid blocking the loop for file I/O.','No onlyactiveconnectionsmemory or guaranteedfile API monopoly.',ur+op)
f(11,'prompt','A Linux team submits many concurrent file reads through glibc POSIX aio_read, expecting no userspace worker threads. Profiling instead finds helper threads executing blocking reads. What explains this?','Hundreds threads not default20; remove unsupported observation count.',ai+ini)
f(11,'explanation','glibc POSIX AIO does not require O_DIRECT. Linux native AIO is a different interface, and direct I/O is not itself a guarantee that submission cannot block.','Native AIO not blanket requirement.',ai,0)
f(11,'explanation','Correct. glibc uses helper threads for this interface. That explains the execution mechanism; performance relative to a custom pool still requires measurement.','No guaranteed no advantage.',ai,1)
f(11,'workedExample','1. aio_read queues a request in the glibc implementation.\n2. A helper thread performs the underlying I/O; excess requests can wait for available workers.\n3. Completion status is collected separately through the POSIX AIO interface.\n4. aio_init documents a default maximum of 20 workers, adjustable by configuration; a claim of hundreds would require additional evidence.\n5. io_uring offers another completion interface but can also use workers. Compare supported operations and measured performance rather than labelling thread-based completion fake.','Defaultworker20 and interface vsimplementation.',ai+ini)
